<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<kml xmlns="http://www.opengis.net/kml/2.2">
  <Document>
    <name>🌌 LIVE GLOBAL AURORA FORECAST</name>
    <description><![CDATA[<p><b>Legend</b><br><img src="https://wyattleathorn7.github.io/great-lakes-live-environment/aurora_global/legend.png" width="600" alt="legend"><br>Global OVATION auroral intensity, FIXED absolute scale 0&ndash;30+: <b>LOWEST 0</b> (transparent) &rarr; <b>6 bright green</b> (oval overhead) &rarr; <b>14 orange</b> &rarr; <b>23 magenta</b> (severe) &rarr; <b>HIGHEST+ 30+</b> (violet extreme). Same intensity always shows the same color. White halo numbers are actual intensity at that spot. White viewline hugs the oval's farthest equatorward edge (dipping where coverage dips, like NOAA's own view line) (<b>~59°N northern-oval farthest reach</b>) — aurora may be visible low on the northern horizon from there under dark, clear skies. The line spans only longitudes where the oval actually reaches near it. Faint equatorial speckles are a known OVATION grid artifact, not aurora.</p><p>This entry auto-refreshes from the live overlay (source: NOAA/SWPC OVATION Prime aurora model (Aurora Viewline product family)).</p><p>Turn on/off independently of all other layers. Add this file once; new source cycles appear automatically.</p><p><i>Not endorsed by NOAA.</i></p>]]></description>
    <NetworkLink>
      <name>🌌 LIVE GLOBAL AURORA FORECAST — auto-refresh</name>
      <Link>
        <href>https://wyattleathorn7.github.io/great-lakes-live-environment/kml/live/Global_Live_Ovation_Aurora_Forecast.kml</href>
        <refreshMode>onInterval</refreshMode>
        <refreshInterval>3600</refreshInterval>
      </Link>
    </NetworkLink>
  </Document>
</kml>
